A||||3||MrPBody||The Duke's new head (recapitulated)||||1||
Z||000000||MrPBody||04-04-2001||10:30 PM||||Having seen a few recent posts regarding 4-cylinder head gaskets, I’ll tell my story for the enlightenment of posterity.  This will be a long post.  (Sorry!)<P>My car is an ’85 SE 4-cylinder 5-speed.<P><BR><B>Symptoms:</B>  slow coolant loss with no apparent leak, steamy exhaust on morning start-up; otherwise ran OK.<P><B>Suspected problem:</B> head gasket leak between combustion chamber and water jacket.<P><B>Intended solution:</B> recondition my spare head, “improve” intake and exhaust ports, install reconditioned head with new gaskets and head bolts.<P><BR><B>Why new head bolts?</B>  You must replace all original head bolts with new-design torque-to-yield bolts.  Original bolts were weak -- so I’ve heard –- and the new design bolts are intended for only one use.  (Disposable hardware, I guess.)  Whereas old bolts were to be torqued to a specified value, new bolts are to be torqued to a lower specified value then turned through an additional angle, stretching the bolts.  Sounds fishy, but it seems to work.<P><BR><B>How did it go?</B>  With difficulty.<P>Dover Cylinder Head found that my spare head was cracked.  (“They all do that.”)  Rebuilt head -- $200.  Plus gasket set and head bolts.  (Fel-Pro)<P>I matched the new head’s  intake ports to the intake manifold gasket  and streamlined the valve guides.  ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html</A> )  Tool of choice: Dremel with round-nose high-speed steel and carbide burrs.   Surprisingly, the high-speed steel burr worked fine with the Duke’s cast iron head.  The smaller carbide burr cut slower but left a finer finish.  In spite of using a home-grown valve seat shield, I dinged a valve seat slightly when the Dremel ran amok; however, I was able to remove the blemish with valve grinding compound.<P>With a ready replacement head, this should be a one-weekend job.  However. . .  When removing the head, I broke a head bolt. ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006261.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006261.html</A> )  The cylinder head was stuck to the bolt. ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009021.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009021.html</A> )  After wedging and prying the head off ( <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009150.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009150.html</A> ), rain, babysitting, social demands, daily job, <I>etc.</I>, it took three weekends.  But it’s <B>done</B>, damn it!  It’s done!<P>The old head gasket looked terrible, and probably was leaking in multiple places.<P><BR><B>Screw-ups:</B><P>I forgot to remove the plug from the fan switch hole in the left front (exhaust) side of the head.  It’s a shallow odd-size square plug and it’s in <I>tight</I>!  I may have to substitute a manual fan switch.<P>I may have damaged a valve stem seal when reassembling the head.  The engine emits a sizeable puff of smoke when moving away from a prolonged idling stop.  I’ll give this a few weeks; maybe it’ll go away.<P><BR><B>Advice:</B><P>Get a manual if you don’t already have one.  Haynes is pretty good.  Helm (factory) manuals have more detail but apply to only one model year.  Torque values and other specifications can be easier to find in Haynes.<P>Torque-to-yield can be a pain in the neck.  You will need a torque angle gauge and you may need a longer pull bar (flex handle).<P>Unless you <I>really</I>  need to remove it, leave the throttle body attached to the intake manifold.  Otherwise, you will have to replace the base gasket / insulator.  Good luck finding one on Sunday afternoon.  On the other hand, exhaust passages under the throttle body probably are cruddy.  You could rebuild the throttle body (kit: $50, including base insulator) and clean out the passages.<P>Pay attention to that fan switch plug!<P><BR><B>Outcome:</B><P>It runs again!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I no longer have to drive my father-in-law’s sluggish Nissan King Crab pickup that reeks of stale cat-crap.  (But I’m grateful for the loaner, really!)<P>My subjective gluteal-shear accelerometer detects a little more push at high revs –- or what passes for high revs in a Duke –- with no loss of low-end response.  Was porting worthwhile?  For me, yes -– for the experience if nothing else.<P><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-05-2001||07:38 PM||||Glad it's finally working again.<P>Yes, The old bolts are weak. That isn't a rumor. 5 of them are part of the fire recall for all but 88. That bolt you broke was the one most likely to break. (There is an additional TSB about the bolts beyond the Recall. It specifically mentions that center exhaust bolt.)<P>The new TTY bolts are used in allot of cars. They really do provide a better result when installed as directed.<P>No, You don't want to reuse TTY bolts. They have a nasty habit of breaking if you do that. And they don't always break right away either.<P>Don't be surprised if you still get some steam on a cold start on a cold day. That's normal until the catalyst heats up. Untill then the catalyst and the rest of the cold exhaust are trying to condense whatever moisture has been inhaled by the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||04-05-2001||09:01 PM||||Thanks, Ogre.  Glad to see someone reads this stuff.  Since there have been a few recent posts about Duke head gasket / overheating problems, I thought -- and think -- other Forum readers might find this story helpful.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Turbo1dr||04-05-2001||09:52 PM||||Glad to know it's running again.<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||14
